/*!
    \page qt-add-custom-brush-shaders.html
    \ingroup cmake-commands-qtcanvaspainter
    \title qt_add_custom_brush_shaders
    \keyword qt_add_custom_brush_shaders
    \summary {Prepares custom brush shaders and adds them to the Qt resource system}
    \brief Prepares QCanvasCustomBrush shaders and adds them to the Qt resource system.

    \section1 Synopsis

    \badcode
    qt_add_custom_brush_shaders(target resource_name
        FILES file1 [file2 ...]
        [PREFIX resource_path]
        [BASE base_path]
        [OUTPUTS output1 [output2 ...]]
        [DEFINES "name1=value1;name2=value2"]
        [OUTPUT_TARGETS out_targets_var]
    )
    \endcode

    \section1 Description

    The \l{Qt Canvas Painter} module provides a CMake macro file with the \c
    qt_add_custom_brush_shaders function, which applications can use in their \c
    {CMakeLists.txt} to compile the shaders used by \l QCanvasCustomBrush.

    \note To make the function available, the \c find_package call must request
    the \c CanvasPainter component:

    \badcode
    find_package(Qt6 REQUIRED COMPONENTS CanvasPainter)
    \endcode

    When using \c qt_add_custom_brush_shaders, the build system invokes a Qt Canvas Painter
    preprocessing step followed by the \l{QSB Manual}{qsb} tool automatically,
    and the resulting \c{.qsb} files get added to the resource system
    implicitly. At run time, the application accesses the generated \c{.qsb}
    file via the Qt resource system and passes its path to a \l
    QCanvasCustomBrush.

    \section1 Example

    Assume that we have an application that wants to render with a custom brush
    whose fragment shader is implemented in \c{brush1.frag}. The
    QCanvasCustomBrush refers to \c{brush1.frag.qsb}. To ensure this \c{.qsb}
    file gets generated at build time:

    \badcode
    find_package(Qt6 REQUIRED COMPONENTS CanvasPainter)

    qt_add_executable(exampleapp
        main.cpp
    )

    qt_add_custom_brush_shaders(exampleapp "exampleapp_custombrush_shaders"
        PREFIX
            "/shaders"
        FILES
            brush1.frag
    )
    \endcode

    The above is sufficient to enable the application to access
    \c{:/shaders/brush1.frag.qsb} at run time:

    \code
        QCanvasCustomBrush customBrush(":/shaders/brush1.frag.qsb");
    \endcode

    The original Vulkan-style GLSL source code (\c{brush1.frag}) is not included
    in the application's executable and does not need to be shipped. If there
    are errors in the shader code, the compiler messages are printed at build
    time and the build fails. When changing the shader source file, the changes
    are picked up automatically in the next build, like they would for C++ and
    other source files.

    \section1 Relationship to qt_add_shaders

    \c qt_add_custom_brush_shaders is dedicated to shaders that are used with \l
    QCanvasCustomBrush. It performs additional preprocessing at build time
    before internally invoking the standard \l{qt_add_shaders}{qt_add_shaders}
    from the \l{Qt Shader Tools} module.

    \note Shaders for custom brushes must always contain a \c QC_INCLUDE
    statement, either with \c{"customfrag.glsl"} or \c{"customvert.glsl"}, and
    must be added to the project via \c qt_add_custom_brush_shaders. The standard \c
    qt_add_shaders function is not suitable for custom brush shaders, because it
    does not perform the Qt Canvas Painter specific preprocessing.

    See \l{Qt Shader Tools Build System Integration} for the general concepts of
    working with cross-platform shader code in Qt, and \l QCanvasCustomBrush for
    details on authoring custom brush shaders.

    \section1 Target Languages

    \c qt_add_custom_brush_shaders translates the shader code to a fixed set of targets,
    suitable for the graphics APIs Qt Canvas Painter supports:

    \list
    \li SPIR-V (for Vulkan)
    \li GLSL \c{300 es} (for OpenGL ES 3.0 and newer)
    \li GLSL \c 150 (for OpenGL 3.2 and newer)
    \li GLSL \c 130 (for OpenGL 3.0, mainly to support older software OpenGL rasterizers)
    \li HLSL \c{5.0} (for Direct 3D)
    \li MSL \c{1.2} (for Metal)
    \endlist

    Unlike \l{qt_add_shaders}, there is currently no further
    configurability offered for the set of target languages and versions.

    \section1 Arguments

    The type of shader is deduced from the file extension, which must be \c
    .frag for fragment shaders or \c .vert for vertex shaders.

    The first two arguments are positional: \c target is the target the
    generated resources are added to, and \c resource_name is a name for the
    generated resource. The name must be unique for each \c qt_add_custom_brush_shaders call
    within the project.

    \note The \c target passed as the first argument must exist before \c
    qt_add_custom_brush_shaders is called.

    \note Multiple \c qt_add_custom_brush_shaders calls are supported and each must use a
    unique \c resource_name.

    The remaining arguments are keyword based:

    \list

    \li \c FILES - The list of shader source files (\c{.frag} or \c{.vert}) to
    compile. This keyword is mandatory.

    \li \c PREFIX - The resource path prefix under which the generated \c{.qsb}
    files are made available in the resource system.

    \li \c BASE - A base path that is stripped from the generated resource
    aliases, analogous to the \c BASE argument of \c qt_add_resources.

    \li \c OUTPUTS - When the name of a generated \c{.qsb} file needs to be
    different from the source, this list can contain an entry for each item in
    \c FILES, specifying the output file name. This is typically used when one
    shader source serves as the input for multiple \c{.qsb} files that differ
    via \c DEFINES.

    \li \c DEFINES - Defines macros that are active during shader compilation.
    The list has the form \c{"name1=value1;name2=value2"}.

    \li \c OUTPUT_TARGETS - When using \c qt_add_custom_brush_shaders with static libraries,
    one or more special targets are generated. Pass a variable name here to
    retrieve these targets, for example to perform additional processing on
    them.

    \endlist

    \sa QCanvasCustomBrush, {Qt Shader Tools Build System Integration}
*/
